WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.460
- When you go to a theme park

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:04.010
or some adrenaline-junkie
tourist attraction,

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:05.720
you expect it to be safe.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.950
Thrilling, sure, but safe.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:10.250
But how can the companies
that create those rides

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:12.330
test a new one?
Say you've got a new invention

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:14.760
like say, a zipline
that goes round corners.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:17.730
There are a few rides
sort of like it the world,

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:21.010
but they all use different technologies
and work in different ways.

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:24.340
There are standards for
things that are mass produced.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:27.003
How do you test something
that's one of a kind?

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:39.030
You really swing outwards
on that, don't you?

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:43.480
- We, as a company, like to
invent and break and test stuff.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.620
So we've gone through the process

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:48.300
of testing other people's
stuff to making our own.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.280
- Traditionally ziplines
are a cheap amusement ride.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:52.410
They go from point A to point B

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:55.186
and you need to have
a clear line of sight.

00:00:55.186 --> 00:00:57.120
They're different from a rollercoaster

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.890
which is able to go round corners
but has an awful lot of expense

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:03.380
in building the infrastructure
to hold the rails up.

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:05.100
The difference is our
product transitions

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:09.520
from a wire rope cable into
a corner which is rail-based.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:11.400
That allows you to go much further

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:14.150
because a zipline can travel kilometres

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:17.400
and a corner can provide
a lot of entertainment.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.440
We have a patent on the technology
so we've got sole use of it.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.927
Previously, braking systems
were friction-based.

00:01:22.927 --> 00:01:25.660
Our new system has eddy current brakes.

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:27.320
This is a property of materials

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:31.340
that occurs when you move a
magnet through a conductor.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:34.970
I have in my hand an aluminium channel,
that acts as the conductor.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:38.140
If I drop a steel mass down
this aluminium conductor,

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.690
as you would expect, it will fall.

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:45.350
If I now drop a magnet down
this aluminium conductor,

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:47.250
eddy current braking will slow it down

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:50.510
and it will fall at a very slow rate.

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:53.740
We've captured that and incorporated it
into our eddy current brake.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:55.970
That allows you to have
no contacting parts

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:57.460
so there are no wear surfaces.

00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:59.440
So there's no brake dust that's created.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:01.290
The brake is configured such that,

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:04.310
regardless of the rider weight
and wind conditions

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:05.670
and gradient of the corner,

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:09.730
you will always fall at a peak velocity
which we have governed.

00:02:09.730 --> 00:02:12.280
No electronics in there whatsoever.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:16.840
It solely works on springs,
centrifugal force and eddy current braking.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:18.910
- Regulations on rides and attractions

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:20.850
differ massively depending
on where you are.

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:22.410
In some states in the US, for example,

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:25.760
the job of checking that a ride is safe
is left to the insurance companies

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:27.740
who'd have to pay out if
there was an accident.

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:30.480
The only time the government gets involved
is if something has gone so wrong,

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.640
if the company has been so negligent,

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.660
that there might be criminal charges.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:37.950
Other countries, like New Zealand
are a bit more strict.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.540
There is a difference
between foreseeable misuse

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:42.520
and unforeseeable misuse.

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:45.180
If someone gets their phone out on a ride
to take a picture

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.750
and it falls out of their hands and
cracks someone's head open underneath,

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:50.533
well, you should have seen that coming
when you built the ride.

00:02:50.533 --> 00:02:53.370
Someone was probably gonna do that,
you should have put a net down.

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.650
But if someone deliberately
breaks the restraints

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:58.200
so they can stand up
on your rollercoaster,

00:02:58.200 --> 00:02:59.770
well, that's their own fault.

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:02.900
The definitions are deliberately
vague in law, though.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:05.700
Because it's easier to say that
you should do "everything reasonable"

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:09.170
rather than change the law every time
someone invents something new.

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:10.940
- Most countries have their
own national standards

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:14.030
and they are acceptable
ways of doing things.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:15.960
They lay out how you
should engineer things,

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.100
how you should test them.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:18.850
It's important to comply with standards.

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:21.020
It's also important to show
that you've complied with standards

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:22.870
so that people can trace
what you've done and why

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:24.280
through the design process.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:25.860
Generally when you're doing
something new, though,

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:30.030
there isn't a standard that you can use
to encompass the entire product.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:33.330
So what we do is we actually go out
and we look at all the standards

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.440
and we pick bits from
each of the standards

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:37.060
that we think are most applicable.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:39.740
When you design a ride, for
example, a zipline ride,

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:41.800
you work out fast the person's going to go

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:44.380
and how much force they're going to
apply to each of the components.

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:45.980
If you have a safety factor of five,

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:49.260
you multiply the maximum that
the system will see by five

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.430
and you need to design
and test to that factor.

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:53.460
What we need to do, though,

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:57.330
is we try and work out what will go wrong
should any part of the system fail

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:59.240
and part of that failure modes
and effect analysis

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:04.050
is to work out how
people could do stupid things.

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:05.150
Which they will.

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:06.100
You look at the history,

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.580
you look at what people have
done on ziplines in the past.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:09.970
The main thing is to stay involved

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:11.870
right through to seeing
the thing being used.

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:14.993
Spot potential misuses
and mitigate for them.

00:04:19.270 --> 00:04:20.233
- Oh, here we go!

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:24.030
Oh, man.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:26.480
That doesn't look like
much from the outside

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:28.070
but when you swing round that corner

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:29.693
it makes a heck of a difference.

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:32.390
Thank you very much to all the team

00:04:32.390 --> 00:04:33.840
at Holmes Solutions.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.280
You can pull down the description
for more about them and their work.

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:37.910
The first one of these rides is going in

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:40.703
at Pigeon Forge, Tennessee,
sometime this year.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:42.633
Thank you, folks.

